                <div class="fact-value">Rochester, MA</div>
              </div>
              <div class="bio-fact">
                <div class="fact-label">Rank</div>
                <div class="fact-value">Ship Captain</div>
              </div>
              <div class="bio-fact">
                <div class="fact-label">Key Book</div>
                <div class="fact-value">Seal of God (1849)</div>
              </div>
              <div class="bio-fact">
                <div class="fact-label">Legacy</div>
                <div class="fact-value">Pioneer Co-founder (1841&ndash;1860 platform)</div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div style="margin-top:14px;">
              <a href="/pioneers/captain-joseph-bates" class="btn-teal" style="display:inline-flex;gap:8px;align-items:center;padding:12px 22px;border-radius:10px;font-size:0.9rem;">
                <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M20 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-4-4H8a4 4 0 0 0-4 4v2"/><circle cx="12" cy="7" r="4"/></svg>
                Read Full Biography
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bio-content">
            <h3>The Sea Captain Who Helped Lay the Foundation of Truth</h3>
            <p>
              Joseph Bates commanded ocean voyages before he ever commanded a congregation. A retired sea captain from
              Rochester, Massachusetts, he brought to the Advent movement the decisive authority of a man accustomed to
              navigating by the stars and obeying fixed laws &mdash; and he applied that same precision to the Bible.
            </p>
            <p>
              In 1846 he published his first Sabbath tract, <em>The Seventh-Day Sabbath, A Perpetual Sign</em>, spending
              his last money to print it. He sent a copy to James and Ellen White. The result?
              <strong>The Whites accepted the Seventh-day Sabbath</strong> &mdash; and the three of them became the founding
              trio who established the original, living platform of Seventh-day Adventist truth &mdash; the foundation laid between 1841 and 1860, which remains the true standard for all who cling to the light as it was given in the beginning. This is the foundation Bates helped build. It is <em>not</em> the <strong>&ldquo;new organization&rdquo;</strong> (1SM 204) incorporated as the <em>General Conference Corporation of Seventh-day Adventists</em> on April 15, 1904 &mdash; nor the entity now operating under the name <em>General Conference of Seventh-day Adventists</em> &mdash; the state church formed in New Rome, the District of Columbia, in union with the civil powers of the United States.
            </p>
            <p>
              His 1849 treatise <em>A Seal of the Living God</em> went further &mdash; connecting the Sabbath to Revelation 7&rsquo;s
              sealing of the 144,000 and identifying obedience to the Fourth Commandment as the distinguishing mark
              of God&rsquo;s sealed remnant in the last days. It remains the foundational text of Adventist seal theology.
            </p>
            <p>
              Bates was also a <strong>radical reformer</strong>: total abstainer from alcohol and tobacco, an abolitionist
              who refused to eat sugar produced by slaves, and a health reformer decades before the Adventist health
              message was formally articulated.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

