      <!-- ===== SEAL THEOLOGY ===== -->
      <div class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <div class="sh-eyebrow">Revelation 7 &amp; Exodus 20:8&ndash;11</div>
          <h2>The Seal of God &mdash; Then &amp; Now</h2>
          <p>What Captain Bates identified in 1849 is the central issue of the final crisis: who bears the seal of God and who bears the mark of the beast.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="seal-card">
          <div class="seal-header">
            <div class="seal-badge">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 22s8-4 8-10V5l-8-3-8 3v7c0 6 8 10 8 10z"/></svg>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>The Sabbath Is the Seal</h3>
              <div class="sub">Bates&rsquo; central argument from Revelation 7, Ezekiel 9, and Exodus 20</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="seal-verse">
            <blockquote>&ldquo;Remember the sabbath day, to keep it holy. Six days shalt thou labour, and do all thy work: But the <strong>seventh day</strong> is the sabbath of the LORD thy God.&hellip; For in six days the LORD made heaven and earth, the sea, and all that in them is, and rested the seventh day: wherefore the LORD blessed the sabbath day, and hallowed it.&rdquo;</blockquote>
            <cite>&mdash; Exodus 20:8&ndash;11 (KJV) &mdash; The Fourth Commandment &mdash; the only commandment that contains God&rsquo;s name, title, and territory</cite>
          </div>

          <div class="seal-verse">
            <blockquote>&ldquo;And I saw another angel ascending from the east, having the <strong>seal of the living God</strong>: and he cried with a loud voice to the four angels, to whom it was given to hurt the earth and the sea, Saying, Hurt not the earth, neither the sea, nor the trees, till we have <strong>sealed the servants of our God in their foreheads.</strong>&rdquo;</blockquote>
            <cite>&mdash; Revelation 7:2&ndash;3 (KJV) &mdash; The angel with the seal ascending from the east &mdash; from the Sabbath&rsquo;s direction</cite>
          </div>

          <p class="seal-body">
            Bates argued that a government seal contains three elements: the <strong>name</strong>, <strong>title</strong>, and
            <strong>territory</strong> of the sovereign. The Fourth Commandment alone among the Ten Commandments contains all three:
            &ldquo;the LORD&rdquo; (name), &ldquo;thy God&rdquo; (title: Creator), and &ldquo;heaven and earth, the sea, and all that in them is&rdquo;
            (territory). <strong>The Sabbath commandment is God&rsquo;s seal embedded in His own law.</strong>
          </p>

          <div class="compare-row">
            <div class="compare-box seal">
              <h4>The Seal of God — Sabbath Keepers</h4>
              <ul>
                <li>Keep the seventh-day Sabbath (Friday sundown to Saturday sundown)</li>
                <li>Worship the Creator who made heaven, earth, sea</li>
                <li>Obey all Ten Commandments — the character of God</li>
                <li>Sealed in the forehead before the winds of strife</li>
                <li>Part of the 144,000 — commandments of God &amp; faith of Jesus</li>
                <li>Protected through the seven last plagues</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="compare-box mark">
              <h4>The Mark of the Beast — Sunday Keepers</h4>
              <ul>
                <li>Keep Sunday — the day changed by human authority</li>
                <li>Worship the beast&rsquo;s counterfeit sabbath</li>
                <li>Set aside the Fourth Commandment as abolished</li>
                <li>Receive the mark in forehead (conviction) or hand (compliance)</li>
                <li>Drink of the wine of Babylon&rsquo;s fornication</li>
                <li>Receive the seven last plagues without remedy</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

